

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Context, so my questions are grounded: this is a static page ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, plain script tag, no framework, no
  bundler, no dependencies). [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub — [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is never actually called. The [38;5;153msrc/[39m tree (CommonJS, [38;5;153mgreet[39m)
  is unrelated to the webapp. There's no test infrastructure and no linting.

  First question, and it's the one everything else depends on.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the identifier is [3missued by a server[0m or [3mminted by the browser[0m. This is not a naming difference — it
  decides what the ID is worth.

  - [1mServer-issued[0m (the login response carries it): the ID is a real, authoritative user identity. It can be trusted,
    correlated with server-side records, and used for anything that matters. Cost: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m has to stop being a stub, or
    you stub the [3mresponse[0m shape now and wire the real call later. This is the one that fits "a real user identifier."
  - [1mClient-generated[0m (the browser creates a UUID and stores it): works today with no backend, and genuinely persists
    across forms and reloads. But it identifies a [3mbrowser[0m, not a person — clearing storage or switching devices makes a
    new "user," and it can be edited by anyone with devtools. It's fine for analytics, and unsafe as an identity.

  These are expensive to swap later, because every consumer you add in between inherits the trust level of whichever you
  pick.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m server-issued, with the stub returning a realistic response shape so the persistence and
  distribution layer is built correctly now and only the fetch call changes later. Picking client-generated to avoid the
  stub work means the "real user identifier" you asked for isn't one.
